WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.402
-Our next guest is an
Emmy-nominated writer and actor

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:04.604
you know best from her work on

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.105
the critically acclaimed show
"Catastrophe."

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:07.440
You can see her next
in "Together,"

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.741
which is in theaters
this Friday.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:09.875
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.875 --> 00:00:13.179
-I vividly remember
being at dinner with him

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.980
and watching him eat
and just thinking,

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.116
"It's the worst thing
I've ever experienced."

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:19.986
-What?
I'm not -- I'm not a bad eater.

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.920
-No, it's not -- it's not that
you're a bad eater.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.990
It's just like -- It's just
the fact of you eating.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:27.593
Like, watching you eat makes
all the various different

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:31.197
processes involved
more apparent.

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:33.766
You know, like, mastication and

00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:36.936
rending of flesh,
grinding of matter,

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:40.340
just mixing with saliva
and sucking it down

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.344
into your big old meat bowl
of acid to break it down

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:48.148
until it's no more than
a brown sausage of turd

00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:50.783
to be squeezed out by your anus.

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.052
-Please welcome back to the show
Sharon Horgan.

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:55.288
How are you, Sharon?

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:57.590
-I'm okay. What a funny clip.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.658
-It's the --
I watched that clip today

00:00:59.658 --> 00:01:02.028
and was filled with dread
knowing that

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.563
when my wife watches it,

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:07.633
that will be when
she most thinks of me.

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:10.135
-That's exactly what she's
thinking as she watches you eat.

00:01:10.135 --> 00:01:11.704
-Word for word.

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.973
With less style,
but word-for-word.

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:16.576
You -- I feel like you continue
to be our greatest creator

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.177
of content about marriage that

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:21.280
makes us both think
it's awful and worth it.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.682
-Oh, well, thank you very much.

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:25.518
But, I mean,
I can't take credit for this,

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:28.988
'cause this is Dennis Kelly
who wrote this,

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:32.658
who I wrote a sitcom called
"Pulling" with years ago.

00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:35.194
So this is his sort of,

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:38.331
you know,
take on the romantic comedy.

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:40.399
But it's set in the lockdown.

00:01:40.399 --> 00:01:42.434
-It's a beautiful script.

00:01:42.434 --> 00:01:45.671
And he is a playwright, as well.
Is that correct?

00:01:45.671 --> 00:01:47.406
-He is, yeah. Yeah.

00:01:47.406 --> 00:01:51.610
I mean, it was supposed to be
a play, originally.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:52.745
But, you know, obviously,

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:55.881
all the theaters
shut down during lockdown.

00:01:55.881 --> 00:01:57.884
And then they thought,
"You know, well, we just --

00:01:57.884 --> 00:01:58.984
you know, we won't put it on."

00:01:58.984 --> 00:02:01.988
But then he just --
I mean, he --

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.389
The whole reason why
he wrote the play is,

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:07.894
he was so angry about, you know,
the mishandling of the pandemic,

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:09.195
and he felt
it was really important

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:12.365
that we sort of say
it now, because --

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:14.567
Well, you know,
I mean, everyone seemed to

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:18.704
sort of slightly forget
about how badly handled

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:20.172
it was when
the vaccination came along,

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:22.141
because everyone was like,
"Yay, vaccinations!"

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:27.981
And, so, we decided to film it,
and we filmed it in 10 days.

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:30.583
Stephen Daldry directed it.

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:34.821
And, yeah, we went and filmed it
in one house, one location.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:38.023
And we filmed it
during lockdown so, you know,

00:02:38.023 --> 00:02:40.760
there was all of that
to contend with.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.496
But, yeah, he was just so angry.
He had a lot of anger.

00:02:44.496 --> 00:02:47.166
But at the same time,
it is a romantic story.

00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:48.400
-It really is.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.804
It's wickedly funny,
it is deeply sad at times,

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:53.506
and it's tremendously romantic.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.174
And when I heard it
was 10 days, I thought,

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:57.043
"That's incredible that they
turned it around in 10 days."

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:59.445
But then there are -- When I
thought about it, I think like

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:02.681
the first 10 minutes
might be a single take.

00:03:02.681 --> 00:03:03.850
I mean, that's --
-Yeah, it is.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.551
-Like, that's where it
really feels like --

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:07.853
I mean, it is definitely,
you know, a piece of cinema.

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:09.955
But it really is wonderful
watching the two of you

00:03:09.955 --> 00:03:11.724
act in a small space like that.

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:12.792
-I know.

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:14.693
We must have
done that about like --

00:03:14.693 --> 00:03:17.897
I think it was
a 20-minute take.

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:20.033
And we had to do it so many
times 'cause we'd, like --

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:23.303
We'd get to the end
and then we'd mess up

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:25.204
just, you know,
before the final hurdle,

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:28.207
and then we'd have to go back to
the start and do it all again.

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:30.409
But we couldn't film it
like regular TV, you know?

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:31.744
So you couldn't do
your sort of --

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:33.046
you know, your close-ups
and your mids and your wides

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:34.246
and all that kind of thing.

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:37.183
You just had to sort of
film it on the move.

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:40.152
-Was it least equitable
who would screw up

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:43.022
a long 20-minute take between
you and James?

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:44.990
-Yeah, it was, actually.
-That's good.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:46.392
-And thank God,
'cause it just --

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.928
It made you feel so awful
when you'd do a good one

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:51.163
and you could really feel it.

00:03:51.163 --> 00:03:55.601
No, I think we both screwed up
as often as the other.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:56.769
Thank God.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:59.939
-Last we talked,
it was during lockdown.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.607
You have your two daughters,

00:04:01.607 --> 00:04:03.942
and they were maybe
giving you a bit of a hard time.

00:04:03.942 --> 00:04:06.812
I think one of your daughters
maybe was complaining

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:08.347
that your eyes
were too close together.

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:11.984
We went back and watched.
We don't think that's true.

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:13.953
-Well, I had a little operation.
-Okay.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:16.355
-I don't know if you can see --
tiny, tiny scar.

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:18.357
-You just took a little bit in?
-Yeah.

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:20.192
I mean, we got through it,
you know?

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:23.262
I mean, unlike "Together,"
I wasn't in a --

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:24.730
you know, in a relationship.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:28.700
So it was just my two daughters
that I had to contend with.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.106
But I mean, yeah, we're sort of
out the other side.

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:35.641
You know, they're kind of --
I don't know.

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:36.943
Did your kids go back to school?

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:40.313
They did or...
-In the next couple weeks, yeah.

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:41.714
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:43.916
So it's all sort of
all normal again.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:48.120
I missed out on
the last term of my --

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.922
well, the last two terms of
my youngest daughter,

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.657
and then, just as soon as
she goes back to school,

00:04:51.657 --> 00:04:55.094
she told me I can't walk her to
the bus stop anymore, so --

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:57.229
And I've already missed, like,
her whole, like,

00:04:57.229 --> 00:04:59.765
you know, last year,
and now it's just --

00:04:59.765 --> 00:05:01.834
That special time that
we had together,

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.435
that sort of
you-and-me special time,

00:05:03.435 --> 00:05:04.704
where I'd put my arm around her

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:06.506
and we'd tell each other
secrets -- that's just gone.

00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:07.839
That's over.

00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:11.376
-I'm very sorry to hear that.
That's deeply unfair.

00:05:11.376 --> 00:05:13.012
-Thank you. It is. It is.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:16.916
-You mentioned
you knew Dennis as a writer,

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:19.786
but I also heard you
had some hesitation of

00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:23.489
maybe doing a lockdown film.

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:25.824
How long did that
hesitation last?

00:05:25.824 --> 00:05:29.462
-Well, I mean, I --
Yeah, I don't think anyone

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:32.331
who was involved in the film
wanted to do a lockdown film.

00:05:32.331 --> 00:05:33.832
Like, Stephen Daldry
didn't want to direct it,

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:35.334
and Dennis
didn't want to write it.

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:38.471
And James and I didn't want
to -- My light just fell off!

00:05:38.471 --> 00:05:39.472
[ Laughter ]

00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:42.708
-Did it?
We didn't notice or hear it.

00:05:42.708 --> 00:05:45.077
-Really? I thought -- Really?

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:46.378
There you go.

00:05:46.378 --> 00:05:49.248
Actually, I made some toast
just before we started

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:50.516
and I burned it and I was like,

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:54.453
"The bloody fire curtain is
going to come down,

00:05:54.453 --> 00:05:55.921
and the alarm is
going to go off."

00:05:55.921 --> 00:05:58.257
But, no, it's just the light.

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:00.359
But, yeah, no,
none of us wanted to do it.

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:05.130
And then I read the script and I
had this mad reaction to it,

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.433
because, I mean, you know,

00:06:07.433 --> 00:06:08.901
obviously,
it's set in the lockdown,

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:10.937
and, you know, it's about all

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:12.871
the terrible, tragic
things that happened.

00:06:12.871 --> 00:06:16.142
But I just found it, like,
really, really romantic.

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:17.743
And I remember
calling Dennis and going,

00:06:17.743 --> 00:06:19.712
"This is so beautiful.

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:23.048
I mean, this actually made me
cry, because, you know, I --

00:06:23.048 --> 00:06:25.484
You clearly love
your girlfriend very, very much.

00:06:25.484 --> 00:06:27.453
And I -- You know, I'm jealous.

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:29.121
I would love to have
a love like that."

00:06:29.121 --> 00:06:30.957
But anyone else who
read it was like,

00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:32.157
"What are you talking about?

00:06:32.157 --> 00:06:34.259
I mean, it's great,
but it's not romantic."

00:06:34.259 --> 00:06:36.695
You know, he tells her that
he hates her face,

00:06:36.695 --> 00:06:39.065
and she says, you know,
when she thinks about him,

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:40.632
she gets the same feelings
when she thinks about

00:06:40.632 --> 00:06:42.068
her dead dad's cancer.

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:44.837
I'm like, "Yeah, but, when he
says, "I probably love you,"

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:47.206
and, you know, when he says
that their love exists

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:49.442
in the place beyond hate,
and they're like,

00:06:49.442 --> 00:06:52.511
"But, you know,
he tries to kill her.

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:55.247
She wishes
explosive diarrhea on him."

00:06:55.247 --> 00:06:57.382
But -- And there's this --
In the film, like,

00:06:57.382 --> 00:06:58.984
not that it's not
a big spoiler or anything,

00:06:58.984 --> 00:07:03.221
but -- Well, it is a bit,
but he proposes to her.

00:07:03.221 --> 00:07:07.393
And I found out just today that
it was a hidden proposal

00:07:07.393 --> 00:07:10.029
from Dennis to his girlfriend,
because he rang me today

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:13.733
and asked me to be his
best person at his wedding.

00:07:13.733 --> 00:07:16.268
And I was like, "Yes!
I called it!"

00:07:16.268 --> 00:07:19.238
You know, I called it.
I knew it was romantic.

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:20.572
-Well done.

00:07:20.572 --> 00:07:22.708
I did not -- Yeah, I was very
surprised to hear that

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:25.177
this work was also
a secret proposal.

00:07:25.177 --> 00:07:26.345
That would not have been
my takeaway.

00:07:26.345 --> 00:07:27.513
-I know.

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:29.448
It's like the most --
world's most private

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:31.450
sort of Easter egg, I suppose.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:33.753
-I was also watching it and I
was commenting to someone here.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:35.621
I was very happy that
James McAvoy,

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:39.291
who's wonderful in it, as well,
he got to be Scottish,

00:07:39.291 --> 00:07:41.627
got to use his natural accent.

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:42.961
-He should always be -- Yeah.

00:07:42.961 --> 00:07:44.630
-He should always be Scottish.
I agree.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:45.864
-Yeah.

00:07:45.864 --> 00:07:48.600
-Is it true that your kids
give you a hard time

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.535
about your lack of
an Irish accent?

00:07:50.535 --> 00:07:51.770
-Oh, yeah.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:55.274
I mean, everyone loves
an Irish accent, right?

00:07:55.274 --> 00:07:57.676
And when I take my kids
to Ireland,

00:07:57.676 --> 00:07:59.311
they're, you know, charmed by

00:07:59.311 --> 00:08:01.647
everyone except me,
except my voice,

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:03.449
because they don't understand --

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:04.750
They just don't
understand my voice,

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:06.518
because, you know, I've lived in
London for 30 years,

00:08:06.518 --> 00:08:09.088
and it's all -- You know,
it's a bit of a mishmash.

00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:11.790
But I was filming there
in the summer,

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:14.493
and whenever I'm there for any
length of time, it comes back.

00:08:14.493 --> 00:08:17.730
Like, I think I sound pretty
goddamn Irish at the moment,

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:18.931
but then they're just like,

00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:20.967
"Why are you putting on
that stupid accent?"

00:08:20.967 --> 00:08:21.967
You know?

00:08:21.967 --> 00:08:23.902
Anything I do, they're just --

00:08:23.902 --> 00:08:25.137
-It does seem like
maybe you're in

00:08:25.137 --> 00:08:26.839
an impossible situation
with your daughters.

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:28.507
-Yeah, I think
you're probably right.

00:08:28.507 --> 00:08:30.543
-Although, you probably have to
bear some responsibility.

00:08:30.543 --> 00:08:32.811
I mean, you did --
you did raise them.

00:08:32.811 --> 00:08:35.347
-I -- Yeah. I did.
I made them.

00:08:35.347 --> 00:08:38.550
-You got to -- There's got to be
very subtle wig work

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:41.753
in this film to show
the passage of time.

00:08:41.753 --> 00:08:44.756
Did you enjoy that?

00:08:44.756 --> 00:08:48.661
-Well, I enjoyed James'
wig work.

00:08:48.661 --> 00:08:52.798
James got to have a man bun,
which is, I mean,

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:56.569
if anyone can pull off a
man bun, it's James McAvoy.

00:08:56.569 --> 00:08:58.070
I just got to have really --

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:00.906
Because they wanted to show,
obviously, the passage of time,

00:09:00.906 --> 00:09:03.275
because it's over the year
of the lockdown.

00:09:03.275 --> 00:09:06.479
And everyone's hair
went nuts, didn't it?

00:09:06.479 --> 00:09:11.283
And, you know,
so they gave me extensions.

00:09:11.283 --> 00:09:12.718
But, obviously,
to put extensions in,

00:09:12.718 --> 00:09:15.487
it sort of just makes your hair
bigger and bigger.

00:09:15.487 --> 00:09:17.989
So I looked like
a kind of, like, '80s slut.

00:09:17.989 --> 00:09:22.461
And then, you know --
And it feels, like, not right,

00:09:22.461 --> 00:09:24.930
but then you take it
out and you really miss it.

00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:26.332
It's just gorgeous.

00:09:26.332 --> 00:09:28.133
-Well, if we're
talking about '80's sluts,

00:09:28.133 --> 00:09:29.201
I would love to show a picture

00:09:29.201 --> 00:09:30.469
of my work over the pandemic.

00:09:30.469 --> 00:09:32.137
This is not a wig.
This is real hair.

00:09:32.137 --> 00:09:35.107
Be honest. What do you think?

00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:36.976
-That's -- You -- Yeah.

00:09:36.976 --> 00:09:38.978
I mean, it's a little --
You look pervy.

00:09:38.978 --> 00:09:40.445
-Uh-huh.
-You look --

00:09:40.445 --> 00:09:41.614
-Great.
-Yeah, it's very '80s.

00:09:41.614 --> 00:09:42.915
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:46.385
-I mean, I don't -- What is that
look that you're working?

00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:50.088
-It's Steff -- It's
James Spader's character, Steff,

00:09:50.088 --> 00:09:51.757
from "Pretty in Pink."

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:55.661
I don't know if that
made it across the pond.

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.663
-Of course it is.

00:09:57.663 --> 00:10:00.165
I mean, I genuinely --
I like that --

00:10:00.165 --> 00:10:01.466
I mean,
not that particular look,

00:10:01.466 --> 00:10:05.271
but I like a sort of
longer-haired look on men.

00:10:05.271 --> 00:10:06.638
So I just --

00:10:06.638 --> 00:10:08.541
-I don't know if we needed that
qualifier there.

00:10:08.541 --> 00:10:10.176
I feel like...
[ Laughter ]

00:10:12.678 --> 00:10:16.415
Hey, the movie is
really fantastic.

00:10:16.415 --> 00:10:17.650
It is always so good to see you.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:20.252
Please promise
to come in person next time.

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:22.021
-I will. I'm sorry.

00:10:22.021 --> 00:10:24.422
Thanks for -- Because I know
this is a bit ropey, isn't it?

00:10:24.422 --> 00:10:26.525
With the old light falling off
and that whole thing.

00:10:26.525 --> 00:10:27.893
-No, it's just --
You know, and, you know,

00:10:27.893 --> 00:10:29.829
the fact that Sean Penn made
the effort and everything,

00:10:29.829 --> 00:10:31.096
it's just --

